WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.660
Let's talk about designing a
socially critical data science

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:15.170
course. I have for
you a play in 3 acts.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.560
Act One: teaching about
wealth inequality.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.580
Act Two: data analysis
for social action.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.960
And Act Three: specific
resources to help you teach.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:29.825
Here's act one.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:36.760
Welcome to your first
day of my class.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:42.060
The very first thing we do is
that everyone gets one penny.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.730
An actual physical penny.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:48.000
The very next thing that
we do is offer a prediction

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.790
about the following:

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.630
Everyone's going to do one
trade with a classmate.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.620
With your penny.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:57.710
How do you trade?

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.850
You find another student,
you bet against them

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.730
on a random outcome event, like
a coin flip or rock, paper,

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.870
scissors.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.980
And the loser pays the winner

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.520
one penny.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.050
Here's an abstract view of what
my classroom would look like.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.670
Two students would find
each other and pair up.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.680
There's one penny at stake.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.860
They're going to compete
on a random outcome event

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.360
and bet on the odds.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.240
And the loser is going to
pay the winner one penny.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.640
So this happens
across the classroom

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.870
simultaneously with different
students in different pairs.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.480
Before trading,

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.630
we're all equal.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.840
If you were to look at a bar
graph of everybody's wealth,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.150
it looks really uninteresting.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.730
Everybody's got one unit
of money, it's a penny.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.690
And we've ordered these
completely randomly.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:02:00.140
But each agent, as
it were, has a bar.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.360
So the first question
I ask before we even

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.690
do the trade is what's going
to happen after everyone

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.000
does one trade?

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.289
And I'd like you
to imagine that.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.480
You each have a
penny. You're going

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.565
to match up with somebody else.

00:02:14.565 --> 00:02:15.940
You're going to
bet on something.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.750
A coin flip is pretty handy
because you both have pennies

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.475
and the loser is going
to pay the winner

00:02:20.475 --> 00:02:20.975
one penny.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:29.950
So what happens
after that happens?

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.980
Is that half the people
in the room go broke.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.580
And half the people in the
room double their money,

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.810
instantly, with one trade.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:44.920
Then you ask what happens
after lots of trades?

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.590
That starts to look
like this where

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:53.140
you can see that a few people
have most of the wealth

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.120
and there's even
one person who's

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.670
got 5 pennies, which
means they quintupled

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.360
their initial investment.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:06.390
But 71% of the people in
this simulation are broke.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:11.290
Not poor, not
impoverished, broke.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.820
Here's another case
of us running it

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.870
with slightly different rules
and a greater money supply.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:17.930
I like this one a lot.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.400
It shows the same features.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.700
We have a few people
with most of the wealth.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.050
But then you have people
who go below the zero line

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.260
with -20 wealth that
they finished with.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.760
And you might be
saying to yourself,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.800
is that an error in the
graph or what happened?

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.040
Loans happened.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.420
We allowed students to
loan money at interest.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.870
So they could loan four pennies

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.620
with the expectation
of getting five back,

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.900
I think was the rule
for that simulation.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:51.320
And the very first outcome was
that the students constructed

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:52.945
their own subprime loan crisis.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:57.590
Nobody was checking credit.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.230
So I watched one student
hit the same person up

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:04.490
for like five loans, and when
she denied him a sixth loan,

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:07.640
he walked across the
classroom and found

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.620
somebody else who didn't
know him and got more loans.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.120
The second outcome is that the
students discovered the value

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.589
of a distribution graph.

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:20.810
So these bar graphs have
the advantage of simplicity.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.870
But every time we
run the simulation,

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.910
we can't predict who's going
to get most of the money.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:28.360
We just know that it's
going to concentrate.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.220
And I do this on
purpose because I

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.860
want them to see that it's
very difficult to predict

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.580
the outcome of a bar graph.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.560
But if there's some
underlying property that

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.590
says, oh, most of the money
is going to go to the few,

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.662
that's the value of
a distribution graph.

00:04:42.662 --> 00:04:44.370
That one distribution
graph can represent

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.830
so many different situations.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.430
So ultimately, we can't predict
which agents will go broke,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:54.410
but we can predict
that most will.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:58.070
Finally, I think it's important
that a seemingly fair system

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:01.870
can still produce
unfair results.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:04.450
It seems fair because
the coin toss is fair

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.290
and everybody has an equal shot.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:10.150
But it's not fair because broke
people routinely get denied

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:12.250
the opportunity to trade again.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:15.960
If you had money, why would
you trade with a broke person?

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.530
You have a 50% chance of losing
some money and a 50% chance

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.560
of getting no money.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.180
That's it.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:24.270
So broke

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.020
people stay broke and they
stay out of the simulation

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.200
ultimately.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:35.060
Here's Act Two: data
analysis for social action.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:38.000
So when I was teaching
at Michigan state,

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.180
a very local crisis was getting
international attention,

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.760
and it had to do with the
poisoning of the water supply

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.060
in Flint, Michigan.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.660
Flint is about an hour
away from East Lansing,

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:52.640
which is where I was teaching.
The super short version

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.160
is that to save money,
the city of Flint

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.340
decided to stop importing
water from the Great Lakes

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:00.510
and divert the
Flint River instead.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:03.080
And the Flint river's
toxic corrosiveness

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.190
leached lead out of the pipes.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.860
This was happening while
we were teaching the course

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.340
and a data set had just
come out from folks

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.100
at Virginia Tech who had
sent at-home testing kits

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:24.020
to 270 families in Flint to get
readings on their lead water levels.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:28.130
And we had students analyze
this and write about it.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.440
Here's one example
of a student analysis

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.570
where what they've
done is try to compute

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:36.890
the number of samples that
exceed the EPA'S action limit.

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:40.160
So the EPA says if more
than 10% of your samples

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.020
are above this
threshold, which happens

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:47.840
to be .015 milligrams per liter,
I think, you've got a problem.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.690
And a student wrote some
code to count up how many

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:54.110
of the raw samples there are
that exceed that amount,

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:57.250
as well as the percentage
that exceed that amount.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.000
The other thing I want
to point out to you

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.430
is it's difficult
to see perhaps,

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:04.740
but that distribution
has a real long tail.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.410
And there are some folks
out there at 0.2 --or, sorry--

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:12.640
0.12 or 0.14

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.920
6 to 8 times the
legal limit for lead.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:19.170
That's a huge problem.

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:23.070
So we had our students write
to the governor of Michigan

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:25.030
with their analysis results.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:26.010
And here's an example student one.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.560
"I constructed a histogram.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.080
"I also wrote a simple
program to compute

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.830
"the number of samples and the
percentage above the limit.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.770
"45 samples exceeded
the EPA limit,

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:42.330
"making this 16.6% of the
samples above the action limit.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:45.960
"This indicates the seriousness
of this problem and exactly

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.520
why we need to urgently
take action to help Flint."

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.740
Act Three: resources
to help you teach.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:00.160
You might be asking,
what can I do?

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:01.870
So I have three pieces
of advice that I'm

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:03.610
going to go into detail on.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:07.930
The first is try to choose
data and activities that

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:11.670
question systems of power.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:15.960
The second is do what you can
to make bias issues in your data

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.950
known and check for them.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:22.650
And the third, which I think
is arguably the most important,

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:26.910
is to adopt a critical
framework to analyze and expose

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:27.730
oppression.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:33.400
So first up, you should
use the opportunity

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.500
of being able to choose
data sets for your class

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:41.390
to analyze, to ask what issues
demand our attention socially.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:43.309
Now, I don't want to
stand here and say

00:08:43.309 --> 00:08:46.070
that every activity that you
do must always be focused

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:47.840
on improving the social good.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:50.540
But I think even if you
started with one activity,

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.670
you'd find it has
a ripple impact,

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:55.400
and it gets you thinking about
more data that can do this,

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:56.960
and it gets your
students thinking

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.870
about more ways they
can try and help society

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.533
through the newfound
powers they're

00:09:01.533 --> 00:09:02.575
learning in your courses.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:08.720
Second, you can check
data for biases.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:12.940
You can ask "Who is being
disadvantaged in this data?"

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:15.470
And so,

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:18.300
A tool for that is IBM's AI 360

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:21.050
I've got the
link down there,

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:22.907
but also I'll have a
QR code you can scan,

00:09:22.907 --> 00:09:25.490
which has all the links that are
related to this presentation,

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:27.980
so don't panic.

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:30.380
The AI fairness 360
has some demo data

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:33.470
that you can take a look
at, and this is an example

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:35.310
of what it can produce for you.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.520
This data was analyzed and
has a privileged group,

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:41.400
which happened to be females
and an unprivileged group,

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.370
which happened to
be males because we

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:47.010
have representational
issues of unequal weighting.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.770
So the tool goes on
to determine things

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:52.760
like what would the statistical
parity difference be?

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.660
What's the equal
opportunity difference?

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.620
What's the average
odds difference?

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:01.970
And then it goes so far
as to offer strategies

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:05.790
for addressing that bias.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:07.770
These are two of the
automated strategies

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:11.000
that it proposes: reweighting
and optimized pre-processing.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.170
I think a third is
adversarial learning,

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:15.960
but I don't know that much
about adversarial learning, so

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.760
don't quote me on that.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:19.650
But the idea is
that the tool lets

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:23.670
you see where your data might be
leading you to conclusions that

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:28.570
are steered by biases
that in most cases,

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:32.190
we want to correct for, and
it offers automated solutions

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:32.930
for doing so.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:40.820
My third point is about
adopting a critical framework.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:43.880
I think it's so important
to recognize that data

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:47.870
analyses are not value neutral.

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:50.480
So this is a book called
Data Feminism that came out

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.730
in 2020 from MIT Press.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.190
You can read the
entire thing for free

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:58.160
at their website, which again
will be in my list of links

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.530
in my QR code thing.

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:03.860
Here's a quote from a
description of the book.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:06.320
"Illustrating data
feminism in action,

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:08.900
"D'Ignazio and Klein
show how challenges

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:11.780
"to the male/female
binary can help

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:15.350
"challenge other hierarchical
(and empirically wrong)

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.400
classification systems."

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:19.930
It's a little jargony.

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:21.720
So let's do this.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:25.200
Raise your hand if you've
either seen or yourself done

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.685
gender as a dichotomous
variable (0 or 1).

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:34.990
Right, so what happens when
your gender is not captured

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:37.870
by 0 or 1 or male or female?

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.860
Well, you could add
another option like

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:44.050
non-gender conforming.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:46.510
But what happens if your
gender is just non-binary

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:48.520
or your gender is gender fluid?

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:50.090
You could add
options until the cows

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:50.660
come home.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:51.220
Really,

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:54.760
what's being revealed to
you is an oppressive system

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:59.620
that is forcing people
into categories that they

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:03.270
don't necessarily identify with
or is forcing them to exclude

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:05.020
themselves from the
data analysis entirely

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:07.440
just by not answering
the question.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:10.830
And D'Ignazio and Klein are trying
to point out it's stuff

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:14.430
like that that we might not even be
consciously aware of if we're

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.780
not thinking for it.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:17.890
That can have huge
impacts on the people

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:19.475
we're asking questions of.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:26.590
My final example comes
from mapping Detroit.

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.980
I call this redlining in action.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:31.590
This was a map that
was produced in 1929

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:34.620
by an all-white, all-male
Chamber of Commerce

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:39.160
and a majority white/male
Federal Home Loan Board.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:41.290
The purpose of the
map was to figure out

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.630
neighborhoods where it
was risky to offer people

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:48.820
loans and neighborhoods where it
was safe to offer people loans.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:52.720
The effect of the map is
that an already racially

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:56.740
segregated demographic
is now disproportionately

00:12:56.740 --> 00:13:00.940
affected by people's decisions
not to lend to, for example,

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:02.710
Black people.

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:05.510
Because

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:09.790
Excuse me, because those
were deemed neighborhoods

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:10.940
where it was risky to lend.

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:13.660
So can you imagine if your
credit worthiness depended

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:18.370
not on yourself, but
on who's next to you?

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:21.490
Imagine how that
crushes social mobility.

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:24.195
Imagine how that
reifies social order.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:30.140
D'Ignazio and Klein say "Early
20th-century redlining maps

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:34.350
"had an aura very similar to the
'big data' approaches of today.

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:36.600
"These high-tech,
scalable 'solutions'

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.590
"were deployed across the nation,

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:40.650
"and they were one
method among many

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:43.020
"that worked to
ensure that wealth

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:48.510
remained attached to the
racial category of whiteness."

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:52.050
That wealth remained attached
to the racial category

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:53.890
of whiteness.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.450
I think it's worth
asking if that's

00:13:55.450 --> 00:14:00.660
what data science was doing
then, what's it doing now?

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.480
Thanks, folks.

